<http://data.silknow.org/event/8ce932f2-1d94-3ba3-924e-5aa110494f8f>
        a                              ecrm:E8_Acquisition ;
        rdfs:comment                   "Circ. 508 to 514 - 1919 formed part of the original  purchase from General Wallace (the donor) of 13 Shan weft-ikat textiles for £52. They were returned to the department in 1979.\n\nExtract from the donor's letter September 4 1919:\n\" In 1886 I was present when we occupied the Southern Shan States of Upper Burmah and purchased some cloths in the villages near Fort Stedman, then the headquarters of the South Shan States Administration\"."@en ;
        ecrm:P22_transferred_title_to  <http://data.silknow.org/actor/f0577f91-f887-3019-bf88-f9e5ba019390> ;
        ecrm:P24_transferred_title_of  <http://data.silknow.org/object/a112d501-4524-36b0-91bd-754f4ac4879f> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "Circ. 508 to 514 - 1919 formed part of the original  purchase from General Wallace (the donor) of 13 Shan weft-ikat textiles for £52. They were returned to the department in 1979.\n\nExtract from the donor's letter September 4 1919:\n\" In 1886 I was present when we occupied the Southern Shan States of Upper Burmah and purchased some cloths in the villages near Fort Stedman, then the headquarters of the South Shan States Administration\"."@en .

<http://data.silknow.org/object/a112d501-4524-36b0-91bd-754f4ac4879f/observation/1>
        a                   crmsci:S4_Observation ;
        ecrm:P2_has_type    <http://data.silknow.org/observation/technical-observation> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note    "A rectangle formed with two identical panels hand stitched along the weft edge. One end with knotted silk fringe.\n\nThe lower part woven in coloured silks; the upper in cotton, with a small quantity of silk.\n\nThe upper part has a thin check in yellow, pale-blue, and red and white silks, on a chestnut coloured cotton and silk ground. The end is turned back and sewn to admit a running string.\n\nThe lower part has many horizontal bands, broad and narrow, decorated with a great  variety of geometrically-treated motives including fret, hook, dot, cloud and floral motives, chiefly in white, purple and shades of green, yellow and red."@en ;
        crmsci:O8_observed  <http://data.silknow.org/object/a112d501-4524-36b0-91bd-754f4ac4879f> .

